  Notarization requires the hardened runtime, and the hardened runtime refuses
  to send Apple Events unless the app asks for this entitlement. Sandfort drives
  UTM entirely through Apple Events: starting a VM, stopping one, and removing
  old registrations during Rebuild.

  Measured, not assumed. A signed app bundle sending the same Apple Event that
  UTMRegistryController sends:

    hardened runtime, no entitlement   -> -1743 errAEEventNotPermitted, no prompt
    hardened runtime, this entitlement -> delivered normally

  Without it a notarized build installs, launches, and never starts a VM. The
  failure is silent by design: -1743 is treated as "the user declined
  Automation", so the app falls back rather than reporting an error.

  Nothing else belongs here. Sandfort is not App Sandboxed, and no other
  hardened-runtime exception is needed: downloads use URLSession, disks and ISOs
  are written with Foundation, and UTM is launched through NSWorkspace.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
